===Golden Skin Strategies===
*'''For the first quest''', play normally until you see {{CI|Twisted Soulvester|name=true}} on the [[Floor]]. Then, either encounter him normally or ask the distractor to bring Twisted Soulvester to the [[Elevator]]. It is easier to do this when he's on the [[Daily Twisted Board]]. Additionally, you should only play until around Floors 5-10, as those are when {{Type|Uncommon}} [[Twisteds]] are the most common.
*'''For the second quest''', you should do a run with a good distractor or have friends that are willing to carry you, while having a [[Halloween Toon]] or {{CI|Connie|name=true}}. It is advised to only do [[Floors]] up to Floor 5-10, as the game gets progressively harder and it will take longer to do a Floor.
*'''For the third quest''', have two friends play as {{CI|Ginger|name=true}} with {{TI|Fancy Purse|name=true}} equipped, then protect one and have them get hit, repeating the process until you can't use your ability anymore. Then, let the Gingers heal you so you can use your [[Ability]] again, making the quest finish quickly. It is highly recommended to do this with {{TI|Soul Sword|name=true}} equipped so you have an extra use of your ability before needing a heal.
==Strategies==
Soulvester has the best extraction in game, with 5{{Star}} in both [[Skill Check]] and [[Extraction Speed]], along with a very strong Ability that can save teammates. However, this comes at the cost of being one of the worst [[Toons]] in terms of mobility, having 1 Star [[Movement Speed]] and [[Stamina]], matching mobility [[Stats]] with {{CI|Shrimpo|name=true}}. Therefore, playing recklessly is much more risky than it usually would be, so caution and patience will be key to playing Soulvester right.

Soulvester may be best used in a team with a distractor present. This will allow him to fully utilize his high extraction stats and keep him away from danger, though his life could be in jeopardy if the distractor dies. In general, caution should be taken with faster [[Twisteds]] like {{CI|Twisted Shelly|name=true}} and {{CI|Twisted Toodles|name=true}} as it can be hard to escape them. A good strategy is to always check corners and behind walls, and position your camera in a position where you can easily spot [[Twisteds]] while doing Machines.

Where Soulvester can really shine outside of his incredible extraction is his [[Ability]], {{AI|Soulvester|name=true}}. Soulvester sacrifices a {{Heart}} to protect a teammate (or himself in a solo run) from one Twisted attack, preserving their Hearts (or the rest of his own Hearts). When his Ability is used, the targeted Toon is given {{Debuff|Guarded}} (and constantly highlighted to Soulvester), while Soulvester himself gets {{Debuff|GuardStance|I}} (speed increased to 12.5 walking/22.5 running); both of these [[Buff]]<nowiki/>s will be removed when the Toon with Guarded is attacked by a Twisted. Take note that the indicator icon above your Ability button will tell you whether the Guarded Toon is in a chase or not, the background will turn red in a chase; valiant music will also play during the Guarded Toon's chase, fading out once the chase has ended. After Soulvester's Ability successfully defends from an attack and he teleports to the defended Toon, he gains {{Debuff|GuardBurst}} (speed increased to 17.5 walking/27.5 running) for 2 seconds and {{Debuff|Untargetable}} for 3 seconds; use this time to get away from Twisteds or get into the [[Elevator]] if it's [[Panic Mode]]/almost Panic Mode. When hit by a Twisted normally, Soulvester gains {{Debuff|HurtBurst}} (increasing speed to 15 walking/25 running) for 2 seconds.

Twisteds are stunned for an extra 1.5 seconds (stacked with their attack cooldown) after attacking a defended Toon, so it could be a viable strategy to use the Guarded Buff and get attacked on purpose to stun particularly dangerous Twisteds like {{CI|Twisted Pebble|name=true}}. In general though, be careful about giving your Hearts out too quickly (unless there is a healer Toon who can heal you, though this could potentially be a waste), as not only could you end up giving your Hearts to players who die quickly despite the protection; you can put yourself on 1 Heart too quickly (not only leaving you in danger, but also preventing you from defending your teammates since the Ability can't be used on 1 Heart). If you receive a protection from a Soulvester teammate, be careful about how you use it, since you could end up wasting it and endangering him when he teleports to you. {{CI|Goob|name=true}} can be a good teammate to have on a team, as he can pull Soulvester away from a Twisted after stunning them. Please note that {{Type|Lethal}} Twisteds will still kill a Guarded Toon. If Soulvester is far from the Elevator, without the means to make it back during Panic Mode, it's a viable strategy for the Guarded Toon to get attacked by a Twisted near the Elevator on purpose, as Soulvester will get teleported to safety.

When guarding someone, keep in mind that you can only guard one person at a time, and each Toon can only have one Soulvester guarding them (the Ability will not work if another Soulvester is already guarding your target). If you change who you are defending, or the person you're currently defending leaves the game, you will not be refunded any spent health, so you have to be very careful to not waste it.

Guarded Toons are immune to being hurt by {{CI|Twisted Goob|name=true}} and {{CI|Twisted Scraps|name=true}}'s ranged attacks, making them much less of a threat, especially Twisted Scraps, as she doesn't have Goob's longer attention span (and ability to displace) which should still be noted. Taking this into consideration, Guarded Toons can safely bait ranged attacks from Twisted Scraps/Goob to put the Twisted(s) on cooldown if they're near the [[Elevator]]; the Guarded Toon can also force the ranged attack cooldown if they and/or their teammates are about to be cornered, allowing for an escape, especially with the stun.

Using the Ability on distractors (especially {{CI|Looey|name=true}} on 1 Heart), {{Type|Main}} Toons, and healers can be a good choice as they are invaluable to the team or vulnerable. Healers can heal you up after you protect them or another Toon too, but be careful since you could eat through healers' Abilities (which all require either Hearts or {{Tape|name=true}}, limited resources). Additionally, with Looey, he can get to 1 Heart and get protected to basically have 2 Hearts, making distracting really easy. Do remember that since Soulvester will teleport, he could take aggro away from distractors after the Untargetable runs out, so equipping {{TI|Diary|name=true}} can be a good idea if you intend on defending any 3 Star Stealth distractors. Also note that this teleport can pull him away from a Machine he's extracting, which could be a bit jarring. The Twisted stun could potentially also screw up a distractor, but it may be worth it if it means saving their life. Getting into the Elevator as soon as possible during Panic Mode will prevent you from being teleported far away if the Guarded Toon gets attacked. If you block damage from within the Elevator, your Ability will be on cooldown until the next Floor. 

Like {{CI|Connie}}[[Connie|his sister]], Soulvester has more advantages in [[Floor Events]] compared to other Toons, being Light-Producing in [[Blackouts]] and possessing immunity to the {{Debuff|Illness|III}}, {{Debuff|Slow|I}}, and Ichor Stains applied by [[Ichor Leaks]] (as well as immunity to {{CI|Twisted Finn|name=true}}'s Ichor Puddles). Overall, he's a nice option if Floor Events are difficult.

If you regularly play Connie and adapt to her similarly poor Movement Speed, transitioning to Soulvester could be a good idea, because you'd not only know how to deal with his 1 Star Movement Speed, but also receive the bonuses mentioned before (max extraction, stunning Ability, speed increasing Ability, etc.). Remember this comes at the cost of less survivability compared to Connie (2 less Stars in both Stamina and Stealth compared to her, plus no invisibility Ability), as well as a more support oriented play-style so be aware.

Due to his low mobility, you may consider spawning {{CI|Twisted Dyle|name=true}}. However, it would be quite difficult. His awful mobility will not just be hard to manage against Twisted Dyle, but also the {{Type|Common}} Twisteds that spawn alongside him. However, his max extraction can make the Floor go faster, so he could be an asset, though wandering too far from the Elevator would be risky, so it is advised to have the faster/stealthier Toons disperse while you finish up the closer Machines. When completing [[Dyle's Floor]], you will finally gain the needed Stamina. The extra Stamina can allow him to use his speed boost from his Ability more often too.

If intending to play in a solo run as Soulvester, making use of his Ability will take care of his mobility. It's best to save the Ability for when you either are getting low on Stamina, are being chased by an extremely fast Twisted, or are being chased by a Twisted with a really high attention span. By saving the Ability for chases, you'll keep your Health up for longer while being able to stun Twisteds who actually need to be stunned. You could also choose to just use the Ability immediately, giving yourself the equivalent of 2 Star Movement Speed near constantly, but note that this can mean putting yourself on 2 Hearts (effectively becoming a Main Character Stat-wise) or even 1 Heart. Even though in solo runs Soulvester doesn't do a cutscene for defending from an attack, he still will get the Untargetable Buff for 3 seconds, which can let you escape more easily when combined with the stun. Either way, a lot of patience is required when soloing as Soulvester, as the mobility can drag things out, or make it difficult to avoid Twisteds; for this reason, it's recommend to really only solo as him if you have the patience or even actively enjoy playing as lower speed Toons.

The best [[Items]] to focus on are those that can help with Movement Speed and Stamina, in order to remedy Soulvester's minimal mobility. Anything helps, even the extra 25 Stamina capacity from a {{II|Chocolate|name=true}} could mean the difference between life and death. Try to stay consistently stocked up on at least one or two mobility based Items, as his extraction is already extremely potent. Using extraction related Items like {{II|Instructions|name=false}} can be pretty great too, as they'll multiply with his high extraction Stats, resulting in finishing Machines very quickly, just make sure you still prioritize mobility. Staying stocked up on at least one Healing Item is a good idea, so you can support teammates more often (or use the Ability on yourself without as much risk). A {{II|Bandage|name=false}} may be less useful than a Health Kit, since the latter can heal 2 (3 with {{TI|Soul Sword|name=true}}) Hearts immediately while the Bandage provides 1 Heart only, though if you take a Bandage you can use it immediately without wasting it, opening up space for mobility Items; in the end it depends on whether you want an immediate heal after using your Ability, or to have a larger sum of Hearts in storage for multiple Ability usages. Healing Items in general are also just useful due to Soulvester's minimal mobility, with either of the two Items being good to keep on hand, though it's preferable to take Health Kits if there are {{Type|Main}} teammates, since they would otherwise waste the Hearts given.

Overall, Soulvester is a decent Toon to go as, though ensuring you reach higher Floors requires a bit of assistance from other teammates. He is a great supporter who can save those in need, but this does mean sacrificing his own Hearts. He has a few resistances, immunities, and advantages, only really being held back by his mobility. Careful gameplay, awareness, being smart with how you sacrifice your Hearts, and making every piece of Stamina count is the optimal way to ensure Soulvester is able to utilize his strengths.

